.TH USERADD 8
.SH NAME
useradd, userdel, acct \- add and remove users
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B useradd
.I name
[\fI"full name"\fR]
.br
.B userdel
.I name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B useradd
(root) adds a user: it asks for the password twice (hidden), and the
accounts server gives the next uid from 1000 up, a group of the same
number, membership of
.B users
(gid 100), and the home
.BI /home/ name
(mode 0700, an encrypted volume whose key is wrapped by the password).
The full name defaults to the login name.
.PP
.B userdel
(root) removes a user; the home folder stays.
.PP
A login name has 1 to 31 characters: lower-case letters, digits,
.B _
or
.BR \- ;
it starts with a letter or
.BR _ ,
and is not
.BR root .
A password has at least 8 characters and is not the name.
.PP
.BR passwd (1),
.BR su (1),
.B useradd
and
.B userdel
are one program,
.BR /bin/acct ,
under four names (symbolic links); it does what its name says, and run
as
.B acct
it only says so. Each only asks the accounts server,
.BR auth (8),
which decides from the caller's identity.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 otherwise.
.SH FILES
.BR /etc/users ,
.BR /etc/groups ,
.B /etc/secrets
(written by
.BR auth (8)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR passwd (1),
.BR su (1),
.BR users (5),
.BR auth (8)
